<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liverpool</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Liverpool is a port city in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, on the estuary of the Mersey. In the 18th century it became the leading British port in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, and in the 19th century it handled much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a>, as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> imports and emigration to America. Its waterfront was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> from 2004 to 2021.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liverpool.jpg" alt="Liverpool" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Tarleton · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pier_Head,_Liverpool_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3059094.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Liverpool lies on the eastern side of the Mersey Estuary, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a>, about 286 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. It is built across a ridge of sandstone hills. The name comes from the Old English lifer, meaning thick or muddy water, and pōl, a pool or creek, and is first recorded around 1190 as Liuerpul. The reference was to a tidal creek, now filled in, into which two streams drained.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Liverpool was established as a borough in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a> in 1207. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it grew from farmland into a small town of farmers, fishermen and tradesmen and a base for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a>. A tidal creek called the Pool gave ships a natural harbour. Disease and its subordination to the nearby port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> kept its growth from advancing until the late 17th and early 18th centuries. The Old Dock, opened in 1715, was the world's first enclosed wet dock. In the late 17th century the port became heavily involved in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, and it also imported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> for the Lancashire textile mills and became a major departure point for emigrants to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><p>The 19th century brought global economic importance and a run of firsts: the first inter-city railway, the first non-combustible warehouse system at the Royal Albert Dock, built in 1846 under Jesse Hartley, transatlantic steamships and a pioneering elevated electric railway. Liverpool was granted city status in 1880 and in 1974 moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a> to the new county of Merseyside. In the 20th century its position as a seaport made it vulnerable in the two world wars, and economic depressions and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/containerization">containerisation</a> of shipping contributed to decline, which was largely reversed after it was selected as European Capital of Culture for 2008.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Liverpool is a port city and metropolitan borough in Merseyside, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and the core of the Liverpool City Region, which has over 1.5 million people. It has over 2,500 listed buildings, among them Speke Hall, a Tudor manor house completed in 1598, and English Heritage has described it as England's finest Victorian city. The Royal Albert Dock now houses the Merseyside Maritime Museum, the International Slavery Museum and Tate Liverpool. It is the birthplace of the Beatles, and Liverpool Lime Street is the world's oldest still-operating main line railway station.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Liverpool” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lord John Russell, Napoleon III and Gladstone toyed with recognition or mediation until Antietam and the preliminary Emancipation Proclamation of September 1862 ended it; British opinion, proud of ending the slave trade and taught by Frederick Douglass and other Black abolitionists, was against the Confederacy except in <b>Liverpool</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Ships from London, Bristol and <b>Liverpool</b> dominated the Atlantic slave trade, carrying more than two million Africans to the Americas during the century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1781 the crew of the slave ship Zong threw more than 130 Africans overboard, and its <b>Liverpool</b> owners then claimed insurance for them as lost cargo; when Olaudah Equiano brought the case to Sharp in 1783, it became a scandal.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Examples include the Revolutionary Communist Party in Britain from 1944, the Socialist Workers Party founded by Tony Cliff, Militant's effective control of <b>Liverpool</b> City Council in the 1980s, Argentina's Workers' Left Front, and the Left Bloc in Portugal, which won 10.19% of the vote in 2015.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/trotskyism">Trotskyism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>…since gone to Barcelona, Madrid, Bogotá, Santiago de Chile, North America, Sweden, New South Wales, Montreal, New York, <b>Liverpool</b>, Perth and Budapest, and the army has been described as the only set of historic artefacts besides the remains of the Titanic that can draw a crowd by its…</blockquote><a href="/work/terracotta-army">Terracotta Army · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Newton taught himself theology while working as a customs agent in <b>Liverpool</b> and was ordained in the Church of England in 1764 as curate of Olney, Buckinghamshire, a poor lace-making village.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amazing-grace">Amazing Grace</a></li></ul></section>
